{
  "evidence": "TEXT_A: NHS funding +£8bn rising to £28bn; free personal care; build 150,000 social homes/yr; rent controls; restore fuel duty escalator; introduce carbon tax; migration reforms including ending the hostile environment, removing Minimum Income Requirement, safe routes, right to work for asylum seekers. TEXT_B: Raise income tax threshold to £20,000 and cut fuel duty by 20p/litre; scrap VAT on energy and Net Zero levies to cut bills; freeze non‑essential immigration, stop small boats, leave ECHR; eliminate NHS waiting lists with guarantees; recruit 40,000 police; fast‑track brownfield housing.",
  "reasoning": "Voters’ top concerns were cost of living, the NHS, immigration, and housing; Text B directly targets bills and taxes, pledges to clear NHS waiting lists, and takes a restrictive stance on immigration, aligning with the salience and direction of those priorities. While Text A invests heavily in the NHS and housing, its emphasis on expansive climate measures, higher motoring costs, and liberal migration stance is less aligned with the dominant concerns and preferences.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "B"
}
